{"ts":"2026-03-11T01:27:38.125Z","role":"ai","text":"Now I have the full picture. Let me now do a proper analysis against our workspace-hub setup:\n\n---\n\n## Video Summary: Key Findings from Matt Maher's Benchmark\n\n**The video tests**: GPT-5.4, Opus 4.6, Sonnet 4.6, Gemini 3.1 Pro across Codex CLI, Claude Code, Gemini CLI, and Cursor — on a planning benchmark (PRD → feature-complete plan, ~100 features, attention/coverage test, not a coding test).\n\n---\n\n### Finding 1: Benchmark Scores\n\n| Model | Tool | Score |\n|-------|------|-------|\n| GPT-5.4 Extra High | Codex CLI | **95%** (highest ever) |\n| Opus 4.6 | Claude Code (execution mode) | **~92%** |\n| Opus 4.6 | Claude Code (plan mode) | **~77%** |\n| Gemini 3.1 Pro | Gemini CLI | 52% |\n| Gemini 3.1 Pro | Cursor | 57% |\n\n---\n\n### Finding 2: The Hot Take — **Stop using Claude Code plan mode for planning**\n\n> \"If you're using Claude Code, stop using planning mode. When I run models through Claude Code in the actual dedicated planning mode, the scores are worse than when I drop into execution mode and tell it to plan. Opus in planning mode through Claude Code scores like 77% and the same model with no planning mode just told to plan in execution mode scores something like 92%. That's a 15-point swing.\"\n\n**Why**: Plan mode appears to constrain the model's approach. Letting it plan autonomously in execution mode (just instructing it to produce a plan) produces significantly better output.\n\n### Finding 3: Cursor consistently outperforms all CLIs\n\nCursor has a built-in verification pass — after planning, it goes back and checks its plan against the original request for gaps. None of the CLIs do this automatically.\n\n---\n\n## Impact on Our Workspace-Hub Setup\n\nThis is directly relevant given WRK-1014 we just implemented (Stage 4a plan mode ideation). Here's the assessment:\n\n**Good news — our pattern is different from what the video warns against:**\n\nOur Stage 4a uses `EnterPlanMode` / `ExitPlanMode` as a **human-orchestrated ideation gate** — Claude drafts a text plan, iterates with the user, then exits before writing artifacts. We're not asking Claude to autonomously plan within plan mode and then auto-execute.\n\n**Potential risk to watch:**\nThe video finding is that Claude Code's **native plan mode produces worse plan coverage** than execution mode with an explicit \"write a plan\" instruction. This means:\n\n1. Our `EnterPlanMode` tool in Stage 4a may produce a lower-quality first draft than simply instructing Claude to plan in execution mode\n2. The 15-point swing suggests the constraint of plan mode actively hurts planning coverage\n\n**Recommended adjustment to WRK-1014 / Stage 4a:**\n\nThe Stage 4a guidance could note: instead of relying on plan mode's inherent constraint, instruct Claude explicitly to produce a comprehensive plan covering all PRD features — mimicking the \"execution mode + explicit plan instruction\" pattern that scored 92% vs 77%.\n\nConcretely, in `process.md` Stage 4a we could add:\n\n> Note: Do not rely on plan mode's implicit planning behavior. Explicitly instruct: \"Produce a complete plan covering every requirement in the PRD/WRK spec before proceeding.\"\n\n**The two actionable takeaways from the video for our setup:**\n1. In Stage 4a, pair `EnterPlanMode` with an explicit comprehensive-coverage instruction (don't just enter plan mode and let Claude infer what to do)\n2. At Stage 4 exit (4b), consider a self-verification pass: \"Check your plan against every acceptance criterion before writing the artifact\" — this mimics what Cursor does automatically\n\nWant me to apply these refinements to the Stage 4a text we just wrote in process.md and the SKILL files?","session":"7602f476-1cf7-49a5-9cc5-f5a2c569539c"}
